---
import NotBuilt from '../components/NotBuilt.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Convert"
  description="The Jarrow converter will turn a Fabric mod JAR into a NeoForge JAR in your browser. It is not built yet (milestone M3)."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <PageIntro
      eyebrow="Fabric → NeoForge"
      title="Convert"
      lead="Drop a Fabric mod JAR, get a NeoForge JAR back with a report of everything that changed."
    />

    <NotBuilt milestone="M3" what="The converter">
      <p>There is no drop zone here yet, because nothing would happen if you used it.</p>
    </NotBuilt>

    <div class="prose body">
      <h2>What it will do</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          You drop one or more <code>.jar</code> files. Within two seconds the page shows, for each one: mod id,
          name, version, loader, game version, license (from <code>fabric.mod.json</code>), dependencies, and the
          <a href="/docs/tiers/">tier</a> the classifier assigned, with the reason.
        </li>
        <li>You pick the target: NeoForge, for Minecraft 1.21.1 or 1.21.11.</li>
        <li>
          <span class="tier tier-a">Tier A</span> converts in your browser with a progress log. The JAR never
          leaves your machine. The result is a download link that exists only in that tab, plus a report: what
          was remapped, which rules fired, which API calls were rewritten, and what was left untouched and why.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="tier tier-b">Tier B</span> offers "Queue a deep conversion" to signed-in users and shows
          the queue position. The result and its report come back to the page, and optionally to a GitHub Gist
          you own.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="tier tier-unsupported">Unsupported</span> mods are refused, and the page names the reason.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What it will never do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Store, cache, host or link to a converted JAR. Conversions are for your own game.</li>
        <li>Convert a mod whose author asked for it not to be converted.</li>
        <li>
          Call a result green without a harness run. See the <a href="/docs/legal/">license policy</a> for how
          licenses are handled.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>The converter will need JavaScript, because it runs in your browser, and it will say so when it is off.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .body {
    max-width: 48rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
</style>
